 {:seon.task/name "a-silent-provider-ends-on-the-kernels-keepalive"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the stability sweep of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md; the owner: platform stability first). src/seon/model.clj:81-84 reads the provider's HTTP response with no end: no read timeout (right: no clock decides) but also no SO_KEEPALIVE anywhere in src, so a provider whose connection went silent (a dropped NAT entry, a dead peer) hangs the model worker forever. AGENTS section 4 names the kernel's TCP keepalive as the one end for a silent network peer. Correct: the provider's socket sets SO_KEEPALIVE with its idle, interval and count as config keys (seon.config.ai/keepalive-*: units, provenance, what happens at the bound: the read fails with the kernel's ETIMEDOUT, recorded as the call's typed error). Proof: a provider stub that accepts and then goes silent (its peer's packets dropped) ends the call with the typed error once the kernel's probes fail."
